program Two yes/no questions.
note A measurement. For each sample the program asks question A, then question B with A's answer given (from),
note and separately B, then A with B's answer given. It counts the pairs of answers in each order and shows the
note order effects and the QQ gap of the order module (symblic/lang/library/order.sym).
note It measures the applicators it is run with, under these words and settings. It says nothing about people
note or about other prompts. It needs many samples to mean anything: with the default six, one changed answer
note moves a share by 1/6, and a QQ gap of 0 is easily reached by chance.
note Every ask runs at temperature 0.7, because at 0 a local model gives the same answer to the same request, and
note with seed = the sample number, because identical requests in one run are answered once and then reused.
note Four asks per sample: a run's default limit of 400 asks allows 100 samples. The program name and the ask
note names reach the applicators, so they are kept neutral.

import order.
use temperature=0.7.

given question_a: Text = "Is a tomato a fruit?"
given question_b: Text = "Is a tomato a vegetable?"
given samples: List of Int = 1..6.
assert len(samples) > 0 and len(unique(samples)) = len(samples).

for s in samples do
  ask ab_a: Bool "{question_a}" with seed=s
  ask ab_b: Bool "You were asked '{question_a}' and your answer is ab_a. {question_b}" from ab_a with seed=s
  ask ba_b: Bool "{question_b}" with seed=s
  ask ba_a: Bool "You were asked '{question_b}' and your answer is ba_b. {question_a}" from ba_b with seed=s
end.

note The counts of one order, {yy, yn, ny, nn}: the first letter is the answer to A, the second the answer to B.
function tally(a: List of Bool, b: List of Bool) -> Counts = {yy: count(zip(a, b), [true, true]), yn: count(zip(a, b), [true, false]), ny: count(zip(a, b), [false, true]), nn: count(zip(a, b), [false, false])}.
let ab = tally(ab_a, ab_b).
let ba = tally(ba_a, ba_b).

show ab as "counts when A was asked first".
show ba as "counts when B was asked first".
show order.order_effect_a(ab, ba) as "order effect on A: yes share, A first minus B first".
show order.order_effect_b(ab, ba) as "order effect on B: yes share, A first minus B first".
show order.qq_gap(ab, ba) as "QQ gap: share of different answers, A first minus B first".
